WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.827
Defensive design patterns make memory assumptions visible.

2
00:00:03.827 --> 00:00:11.480
C does not prevent every unsafe state, so good APIs and data structures should make safe use easier than unsafe use.

3
00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:19.068
The strongest patterns keep related facts together: pointer, count, capacity, ownership, lifetime, and valid range.

4
00:00:19.068 --> 00:00:24.017
Keep pointers and sizes together in function arguments or structured types.

5
00:00:24.017 --> 00:00:30.483
A function that receives only a raw pointer must rely on hidden knowledge or external assumptions.

6
00:00:30.483 --> 00:00:38.928
A function that receives pointer plus count, pointer plus capacity, or a buffer structure can make its bounds contract explicit.

7
00:00:38.928 --> 00:00:41.765
Distinguish count, capacity, and byte size.

8
00:00:41.765 --> 00:00:45.328
Count describes meaningful elements currently present.

9
00:00:45.328 --> 00:00:47.769
Capacity describes available storage.

10
00:00:47.769 --> 00:00:50.013
Byte size describes memory extent.

11
00:00:50.013 --> 00:00:52.322
A string also has terminator rules.

12
00:00:52.322 --> 00:00:58.656
Mixing these ideas in one loosely named variable makes memory review harder than it needs to be.

13
00:00:58.656 --> 00:01:01.295
Define ownership transfer rules clearly.

14
00:01:01.295 --> 00:01:06.310
If a function takes ownership of a pointer, the caller should stop using it.

15
00:01:06.310 --> 00:01:11.918
If a function borrows a pointer, it should not store it beyond the promised lifetime.

16
00:01:11.918 --> 00:01:18.120
If a function returns allocated storage, the caller should know which cleanup routine applies.

17
00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:21.815
Ownership uncertainty is a common root of heap mistakes.

18
00:01:21.815 --> 00:01:24.124
Centralize cleanup where practical.

19
00:01:24.124 --> 00:01:32.174
A single cleanup path can reduce duplicate frees and missed releases, especially in functions with several failure points.

20
00:01:32.174 --> 00:01:39.431
The cleanup path should still know which resources were acquired, so initialization and state tracking matter.

21
00:01:39.431 --> 00:01:43.258
Use helper functions for repeated bounds and append logic.

22
00:01:43.258 --> 00:01:52.034
A reviewed helper can validate ranges, grow dynamic buffers safely, maintain terminators, and update count and capacity consistently.

23
00:01:52.034 --> 00:01:59.225
Helpers should have precise contracts so they improve clarity rather than hiding risk behind a friendly name.
